ntdll: a program sees the page protection it set, not the emulator's write trap.

FEX makes a program's read-write-execute pages that hold translated code read-execute, so a write into them faults
into its tracking of self-modifying code; Windows has no such step. NtProtectVirtualMemory returned that
read-execute as the old protection and NtQueryVirtualMemory reported it, so a program that saved a page's
protection, changed it and restored what it saved made its own page read-execute and faulted on its next write
(smcprot.exe on the M4, 2026-10-18: VirtualQuery and VirtualProtect read 0x20 for a page the program made 0x40, and
100 of 100 such writes reached the program's handler). Copy protections guard their code pages this way, and
Microsoft Flight Simulator 2024's ran into an unmapped page 45 s into every flight once FEX re-protected pages more
often (smc-retire).

The emulator gains an export, GuestProtection (address, protection): the protection the program set, given the
one the page has. NtProtectVirtualMemory passes its old protection through it before the post-call notification
updates the emulator's view, and NtQueryVirtualMemory (now wrapped like the other memory calls) passes the
Protect of a committed region through it for MemoryBasicInformation. The emulator's own calls skip both, as they
skip the notifications. An emulator without the export changes nothing.

--- a/dlls/ntdll/signal_arm64ec.c
